mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — content to hooks, end to end\n\nThe full method on real content: find the raw material, generate across mechanisms, score, pick.\nVoice here is the blunt indie-founder voice from the voice-builder examples (short, no\nexclamation marks, dry, concrete). Your output uses the loaded `voice.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Example 1 — Caption hook\n\n**The content:** a post about deleting a feature the team spent six months building because\nnobody used it; the fix (a \"boring\" feature) became the most-used one in two days.\n\n**Raw material (Step 2):** the surprising numbers (6 months → 2 days), the stakes (wasted work),\nthe contrarian lesson (build less).\n\n**Generate across mechanisms (Step 3):**\n1. *Specificity:* \"Six months to build. Two days to replace. Nobody noticed the first one.\"\n2. *Mistake:* \"I spent six months building a feature nobody asked for. Classic.\"\n3. *Contrarian:* \"Shipping more features is making your product worse.\"\n4. *Curiosity:* \"The feature we deleted today taught me more than the one we shipped.\"\n5. *Stakes:* \"We torched six months of work in one commit this morning.\"\n6. *Question:* \"What's the feature you keep building because customers 'ask' for it?\"\n\n**Score (Step 4):** All are on-voice and true. #1 and #2 have the strongest *specificity* and\n*pull* and land well inside the ~125-char cutoff. #3 is a strong contrarian but slightly less\ntrue to *this* post (it's about behavior, not feature count). #6 is good if the goal is comments.\n\n**Pick:** #2 for reach (self-deprecating, on-voice, instant story), #6 if the goal is comments.\nRecommend #2.\n\n---\n\n## Example 2 — Video hook (first 3 seconds, muted-first)\n\n**The content:** same story, as a 30-second Reel.\n\n**Deliver all three channels:**\n- **Opening visual:** hand hovering over a keyboard, then hitting \"delete\" on a big block of\n  code — motion in frame one.\n- **On-screen text (sound-off hook):** \"Deleting 6 months of work.\"\n- **Verbal (first words):** \"We just deleted our most-requested feature.\"\n\n**Why it works:** the visual is a pattern interrupt (destroying work), the on-screen text\ncarries the gap muted, and the spoken line adds the contrarian twist (\"most-requested\"). No\nlogo, no \"hey guys,\" hook complete inside ~2 seconds.\n\n**A weaker alternative (for contrast):** opening on the founder's face saying \"Hey everyone, so\ntoday I want to talk about product decisions…\" — the gap doesn't arrive until second 6, by which\npoint the viewer is gone.\n\n---\n\n## Example 3 — Carousel cover\n\n**The content:** a 7-slide carousel on writing hooks.\n\n**Cover candidates:**\n- \"7 hooks that actually work →\" *(specificity + promise of the swipe)*\n- \"Your hooks are why nobody reads your posts\" *(stakes/callout)*\n- \"Stop writing hooks like it's 2019 →\" *(contrarian)*\n\n**Pick:** the callout if the goal is to stop the scroll of people who suspect their hooks are\nweak; the specific \"7 hooks →\" if the goal is saves. Either way, one idea, legible at thumbnail\nsize, promising what slides 2–7 deliver.\n\n---\n\n## What every example shows\n\n- The hook is **extracted from the content's real strongest element**, not invented.\n- Several **mechanisms** are tried before one is chosen.\n- The winner is **specific, true, in-voice, and inside the cutoff**.\n- For video, all **three channels** (visual + text + verbal) are delivered and work muted.\n- The recommendation is tied to the **goal** (reach vs comments vs saves).\n"
